<!--
    kuna issue testcase: a `rep movsd` that copies a by-value struct into the
    outgoing argument area with a known count is expanded before the stack is
    heritaged (`p3_dataflow/kuna_repframecopy.rs`, always on; `indexaliasguard
    off` turns it off with the other stack guards).

    Issue:      kuna GH-1562 - x86-32 MSVC: a by-value struct argument copied
                with `rep movsd` makes the call that takes it print with no
                arguments, and a pushed return address prints as a value.
    Stage:      P3 dataflow - right after the first heritage pass, before the
                stack's first heritage (`ActionHeritage`).
    P0 assert:  none (a strict fix); one pass.

    byval_pe_i386.exe (VC8 /O2, notes in byval_pe_i386.txt), draw (0x401000):
    paint(a, b, 3), then `Rect r = *src` (rep movsd into a local) and
    `fill(r, b)` (`sub esp,0x20; mov edi,esp; rep movsd` into the outgoing
    area). Before: `v9 = 0x401018;` (paint's pushed return address), two
    `while` copy loops and `sub_401070()`. Now fill takes r's eight dwords and
    b, paint keeps its three arguments, and neither the loops nor the return
    address are left.
    repframecopy_pe_i386.exe (clean-room asm, notes in repframecopy_pe_i386.asm):
    ebp_draw (0x401070, frame pointer, a branch before the copies) passes src's
    eight dwords and b to fill8; forward (0x401130) passes its own by-value
    parameter on; the entry (0x401180) passes a global table by value, and its
    other calls keep their pushed arguments. copy_global (0x4010d0, no frame
    slot) is left to `repstring`, which prints it as a memcpy. Left as loops:
    big_pass (0x4010b0, 32 dwords, too large), zero_pass
    (0x4010f0, the copy reads a local a `rep stosd` walks) and loop_fill
    (0x401150, the copy reads a local an indexed loop writes). shift_pass
    (0x401270) moves nine slots at the stack pointer down by one with an
    overlapping copy: fill8 takes 1..8 and 8 (on main the copied slots kept
    their old values). The register-argument callers d_virtual (0x401300),
    d_ptr (0x401340) and d_direct (0x401370) copy a record into a local at the
    bottom of the frame: no call takes it, so each move stays a loop and no
    call gains a stack argument.
    byvalreuse_pe_i386.exe (clean-room, source and recipe in
    byvalreuse_pe_i386.s): byval (0x401000) copies a 32-byte struct into an
    outgoing block that reuses an earlier __cdecl call's 12 bytes of pushes
    (`sub esp,0x14`) and calls a __thiscall method ending in `ret 0x20` whose
    stack effect the purge walk cannot settle: the method takes the eight
    dwords (#14). Three copies whose callee's own returns do not pop the block
    stay loops: cleanup (0x4010d0) calls a __cdecl callee and removes the block
    itself, and keeps reading its second parameter after the call (#15); local
    (0x401100) copies a local at the bottom of the frame before an unrelated
    __cdecl call and reads it back (#16); nested (0x401130) copies f's struct
    and then calls g(0) first, and g takes only its 0 (#17); stray (0x4011d0)
    calls a __cdecl callee whose cold path ends in a call that does not return,
    where the walk runs on through the int3 padding into the next function's
    `ret 0x20`, and keeps reading its second parameter (#18); quit (0x401240)
    passes a block to a __stdcall callee (ret 0x20) whose other path ends in
    exit, which takes it while exit is known not to return, and the walk is
    taken again once `noreturn_known` is turned off: it no longer does (#19);
    settle (0x401280) calls the __stdcall note (ret 4, which the walk proves)
    before the block for take: once take's pop is stated every call has one,
    so note's is stated too and the block is read from settle's parameter
    (#20); unsettled (0x4012c0) calls the __stdcall guess first (ret 4, but
    it calls through a pointer, so the walk cannot settle it): stating take's
    pop would leave guess's pop to the solver, whose guess moves the block,
    so it stays a loop (#21).
-->
